# Rust Code Style

Apply when writing or reviewing Rust. Prefer clarity, explicit failure modes, and small APIs over cleverness.

## Tooling (non-negotiable)

- **MSRV: Rust >= 1.96.0** (keep `clippy.toml` / `package.rust-version` in sync).
- Edition **2024**; format with workspace `rustfmt.toml`; keep Clippy clean (`-D warnings` in CI).
- Use `cargo fmt` / `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings` before finishing.
- Prefer stable Rust. Do not use APIs newer than the MSRV.

## Naming & structure

- Follow Rust API guidelines: `snake_case` items, `CamelCase` types/traits, `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` consts.
- One concern per module; re-export a small public surface from `lib.rs`.
- Prefer `Foo` / `FooError` / `FooConfig` over vague names (`Manager`, `Helper`, `Util`).
- Keep functions short and single-purpose; extract when nesting or args explode.

```rust
// ❌ BAD
fn do_it(x: &str) -> String { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
fn normalize_path(path: &str) -> String { ... }
```

## Ownership & APIs

- Accept `&str` / `&[T]` / `&Path` instead of owned types when ownership is not required.
- Return owned values (`String`, `PathBuf`, structs) when the caller needs to keep them.
- Prefer `&self` / `&mut self`; take `self` only for builders or consuming transforms.
- Avoid unnecessary `Clone`; clone at the boundary where ownership must diverge.
- Use `impl Iterator<Item = T>` / `impl AsRef<Path>` in public APIs when it simplifies callers.

## Error handling

- **Libraries (`memorylake-core`)**: typed errors with `thiserror`; expose `pub type Result<T> = std::result::Result<T, Error>`.
- **Binaries (`memorylake-cli`)**: `anyhow::Result` at the edges; convert/context when crossing into UX.
- Never swallow errors (`let _ = ...` on `Result`, empty `catch`). Use `?`, map, or explicit match.
- Add context at boundaries (`with_context(|| ...)`) so failures explain *what* failed.
- Prefer `Error` variants over stringly errors; reserve free-form messages for truly opaque cases.

```rust
// ❌ BAD (library)
fn load() -> Result<Config, String> { ... }

// ✅ GOOD (library)
#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
pub enum Error {
    #[error("failed to read config from {path}")]
    ReadConfig { path: PathBuf, source: std::io::Error },
}
```

## Option / Result discipline

- Prefer `?`, `map`, `and_then`, `ok_or_else` over nested `match` when readability wins.
- Use `expect` / `unwrap` only for invariants that are locally proven (and document why).
- In library code, avoid panics for expected failures; return `Result` / `Option`.

## Types & data modeling

- Make invalid states unrepresentable (newtypes, enums over boolean flag soup).
- Derive only what you need: `Debug` widely; `Clone`/`PartialEq` when meaningful; avoid reflexive `Default` if empty is invalid.
- Prefer `#[must_use]` on results/builders where ignoring the value is a bug.
- Keep public fields rare; use constructors / accessors when invariants exist.

## Async & concurrency

- Don't add async unless I/O or waiting requires it.
- Hold locks briefly; never `.await` while holding a `std::sync` lock.
- Prefer message passing / structured concurrency over shared mutable state.
- Document thread-safety (`Send`/`Sync` implications) for public types that cross threads.

## Logging & observability

- Use `tracing` (`event!` / `info!` / `debug!`); no ad-hoc `println!` for diagnostics in library code.
- CLI may print user-facing output; keep machine logs structured and level-gated (`-v` / `RUST_LOG`).
- Log with useful fields; avoid logging secrets or huge payloads.

## Documentation & comments

- Every public item gets a `///` doc comment; modules get `//!`.
- Docs explain *why* / contracts / edge cases; code shows *how*.
- Comment only non-obvious invariants, safety notes, or workarounds—not restating the code.

## `unsafe`

- Default to safe Rust. `unsafe` needs a brief `// SAFETY:` comment stating the invariant upheld.
- Encapsulate unsafety behind a small safe API; never scatter raw pointer logic.

## Tests

- Unit tests live in `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` next to the code when local.
- Name tests after behavior: `rejects_empty_token`, not `test1`.
- Cover error paths and edge cases, not only the happy path.
- Prefer deterministic tests; avoid sleep/network unless integration-scoped.

## Dependencies & workspace

- Add crates via workspace dependencies when shared; pin thoughtfully, prefer well-maintained crates.
- Don't pull heavy deps for one-liners you can write clearly in a few lines.
- Feature-gate optional functionality; keep default features minimal for libraries.

## PR hygiene for Rust changes

- No dead code, unused imports, or `#[allow(...)]` without a one-line justification.
- Match existing module layout and error/logging patterns before inventing new ones.
- Prefer incremental, reviewable diffs over drive-by refactors.
